## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft fixed, branchless bit-twiddling routines across 128‑bit values, a common requirement in high‑performance bitmap or Bloom filter code. It exposes knowledge of SWAR reductions and careful handling of two 64‑bit lanes.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD within a register) reductions
- Handling 128-bit as two 64-bit halves
- Fixed-mask sequences and non-branching sums

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned hi = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned lo = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type?  - Can you prove correctness with a small formal argument or tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitboard reasoning, edge handling, and efficient parallelism using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Bitboard edge wrapping and file masks
- Parallel neighbor computation via multiple shifts
- Safe, branch-free bitwise design

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t neighborMask64(uint64_t board) {
    const uint64_t notA = 0xfefefefefefefefeULL;
    const uint64_t notH = 0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7fULL;
    uint64_t east  = (board & notH) << 1;
    uint64_t west  = (board & notA) >> 1;
    uint64_t south = board << 8;
    uint64_t north = board >> 8;
    uint64_t ne = (board & notH) >> 7;
    uint64_t nw = (board & notA) >> 9;
    uint64_t se = (board & notH) << 9;
    uint64_t sw = (board & notA) << 7;
    return east | west | south | north | ne | nw | se | sw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 32-bit board layout?
- How would you unit-test edge cases (corners and edges) efficiently?

